diff --git a/docs/wolfssl.md b/docs/wolfssl.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c15d906
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/wolfssl.md
@@ -0,0 +1,207 @@
+
+# wolfSSL* Crypto Backend
+
+The FDO Client SDK's crypto operations can be backed by [wolfSSL](https://www.wolfssl.com/),
+a TLS/crypto library for resource-constrained and embedded devices, as an alternative to
+the OpenSSL and mbedTLS backends. Select it at configure time with `TLS=wolfssl`.
+
+The development and execution OS used was `Ubuntu* OS version [20.04|22.04] / RHEL* OS
+version [8.4|8.6|8.8] / Debian 11.4` on x86, same as the other Linux reference builds.
+
+## 1. Packages Requirements when Building Binaries:
+
+Same base package set as the [Linux reference build](linux.md#1-packages-requirements-when-building-binaries),
+plus autotools for building wolfSSL from source:
+
+* For Ubuntu* OS version [20.04|22.04] / Debian 11.4:
+```shell
+sudo apt-get install build-essential python-setuptools clang-format dos2unix ruby \
+  libglib2.0-dev libpcap-dev autoconf libtool libproxy-dev doxygen cmake mercurial nghttp2 libnghttp2-dev
+```
+
+* For RHEL* OS version [8.4|8.6|8.8]:
+```shell
+sudo subscription-manager repos --enable codeready-builder-for-rhel-8-x86_64-rpms
+sudo yum -y install https://dl.fedoraproject.org/pub/epel/epel-release-latest-8.noarch.rpm
+```
+```shell
+sudo yum -y install gcc gcc-c++ python3-setuptools git-clang-format dos2unix ruby perl glibc-static \
+  glib2-devel libpcap-devel autoconf libtool libproxy-devel mozjs52-devel doxygen cmake make mercurial perl nghttp2 libnghttp2-devel
+```
+
+## 2. Building wolfSSL and wolfSSL-linked curl
+
+With `TLS=wolfssl`, curl is built against wolfSSL too, so the whole stack runs on
+wolfSSL with no OpenSSL dependency. The [Installation-Script](../utils/install_wolfssl_curl.sh)
+handles both steps; it configures wolfSSL with `--enable-curl`, which provides the
+APIs curl's wolfSSL backend requires.
+
+> ***NOTE***: [Installation-Script](../utils/install_wolfssl_curl.sh) installs wolfSSL and
+> curl at `/opt/wolfssl` and `/opt/curl` by default. To use different paths, export
+> `WOLFSSL_ROOT` and/or `CURL_ROOT` before running it; the script honours both.
+
+**Script usage command**
+
+* Command to install wolfSSL and a wolfSSL-linked curl (defaults to pinned wolfSSL v5.9.4-stable):
+	```shell
+	./utils/install_wolfssl_curl.sh -i
+	```
+	You can also specify a specific version with `-v` (e.g. `./utils/install_wolfssl_curl.sh -i -v 5.9.4`).
+
+The script calls `sudo` itself for the steps that write outside the build tree (`make
+install`, `ldconfig`), so run it as your normal user. Invoking the whole script under
+`sudo` also works, but `sudo` drops your environment by default, so exported
+`WOLFSSL_ROOT`/`CURL_ROOT` overrides would be silently ignored and the defaults used
+instead.
+
+If you are building in a dedicated development environment (like a Docker container) and
+have a system OpenSSL-linked curl installed, you can remove it first so the linker
+doesn't pick up the wrong one.
+
+> ***WARNING***: On a general-purpose desktop or shared server, removing `curl` or `libcurl`
+> packages may unintentionally uninstall other system utilities or desktop environments that
+> depend on them. If you prefer not to remove them, skip this step; see Section 6 for how
+> the binaries still find the right curl.
+
+```shell
+# Ubuntu / Debian
+sudo apt remove curl libcurl4-openssl-dev
+
+# RHEL
+sudo yum remove libcurl-devel
+```
+
+To verify the install, `$CURL_ROOT/bin/curl -V` should report wolfSSL, not OpenSSL, as its SSL backend.
+
+## 3. Compiling Local Dependencies (safestringlib and tinycbor)
+
+The SDK requires `safestringlib` and `tinycbor`. To simplify setup, we provide an automated script that clones, patches (for modern GCC compatibility), builds, and outputs the necessary `export` paths.
+
+Run the following from the root of the repository:
+```shell
+./utils/setup_local_dependencies.sh
+```
+
+## 4. Environment Variables
+
+Add these environment variables to your terminal session or `~/.bashrc` (replace with actual paths as output by the script in Step 3):
+```shell
+# Defaults if you used the install script in Step 2
+export WOLFSSL_ROOT=/opt/wolfssl
+export CURL_ROOT=/opt/curl
+export SAFESTRING_ROOT=path/to/safestringlib
+export TINYCBOR_ROOT=path/to/tinycbor
+```
+
+`WOLFSSL_ROOT` and `CURL_ROOT` may be omitted if wolfSSL and curl are in a location CMake
+probes automatically (`../wolfssl-install`, `/opt/wolfssl`, `/usr/local` for wolfSSL;
+`../curl-install`, `/opt/curl`, `/usr/local`, `/usr` for curl). Set them explicitly if you
+installed anywhere else, or to be sure of which copy gets linked.
+
+`WOLFTPM_ROOT` is only needed for `TPM_STACK=wolftpm` (see Section 8), and only if wolfTPM
+is installed outside the default linker search path.
+
+`OPENSSL3_ROOT` is **not** required here; it's only checked when `TLS=openssl`.
+
+## 5. Compiling FDO Client SDK
+
+We recommend using an out-of-source build (`cmake -B build`) to keep your root directory clean of CMake artifacts. See [FDO Compilation Setup](setup.md) and [FDO Build configurations](build_conf.md) for the full set of options.
+
+Select the wolfSSL backend with `TLS=wolfssl`:
+
+```shell
+cmake -B build -DTLS=wolfssl
+cmake --build build -j$(nproc)
+bash utils/keys_gen.sh build
+```
+
+## 6. Runtime Library Paths
+
+`linux-client` and the unit test binaries are linked with an rpath to `$CURL_ROOT/lib`
+and `$WOLFSSL_ROOT/lib`, so they load the wolfSSL-linked curl even when a system
+OpenSSL-linked `libcurl` is still installed. No `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` is needed.
+
+Do not export `LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$CURL_ROOT/lib` in the shell you build from: tools such as
+`cmake` and `ctest` link the system `libcurl` themselves and will fail to start
+(`version 'CURL_OPENSSL_4' not found`) if pointed at the wolfSSL-linked one.
+
+## 7. Running the Application
+
+Running against FDO PRI Manufacturer, Rendezvous, and Owner components follows the same
+steps as the [Linux reference build](linux.md#7-running-the-application).
+
+## 8. TPM* Support
+
+Currently, TPM hardware attestation is supported for both OpenSSL and wolfSSL backends. Set
+`TPM_STACK=wolftpm` during CMake configuration to use the lightweight wolfTPM library for
+device attestation instead of the legacy `tpm2-tss` dependencies. wolfTPM links against
+wolfCrypt regardless of which `TLS` backend is selected, so `TPM_STACK=wolftpm` works with
+either `TLS=wolfssl` or `TLS=openssl`:
+
+```shell
+cmake -DTLS=wolfssl -DTPM_STACK=wolftpm -DDA=tpm20_ecdsa384 -DAES_MODE=gcm .
+# or, TPM_STACK=wolftpm with the OpenSSL TLS backend:
+cmake -DTLS=openssl -DTPM_STACK=wolftpm -DDA=tpm20_ecdsa384 -DAES_MODE=gcm .
+```
+
+With `TLS=openssl`, wolfSSL is only used by wolfTPM: TLS and curl stay on OpenSSL, so use
+your system's OpenSSL-linked curl. Both OpenSSL and wolfSSL end up linked into
+`linux-client`, which is safe because wolfSSL exports only `wolfSSL_`/`wc_`-prefixed
+symbols; its OpenSSL-compatible names (from `--enable-curl` or `--enable-opensslextra`) are
+header macros and never reach the linker.
+
+The wolfSSL built by `utils/install_wolfssl_curl.sh` (step 2, above) already includes the
+flags wolfTPM needs (`--enable-wolftpm --enable-pkcallbacks`, plus the
+`-DWC_RNG_SEED_CB -DWOLFSSL_CUSTOM_OID -DHAVE_OID_ENCODING` defines) - if you built wolfSSL
+that way, no extra steps are needed. If you're linking against a wolfSSL built some other
+way (e.g. a system package, or your own from-source build), it must be configured with
+those same flags or `linux-client` will fail to link against `libwolftpm.so` with undefined
+references such as `wc_CryptoCb_RegisterDevice`, `wolfSSL_CTX_SetEccSignCb`, or
+`wc_AesCfbEncrypt`.
+
+Build wolfTPM itself (against that same wolfSSL) using the install script (pinned to
+wolfTPM `v4.2.0`):
+
+```shell
+./utils/install_wolfssl_curl.sh -t
+```
+
+Or build it manually per its own [README](https://github.com/wolfSSL/wolfTPM#building-wolfssl).
+If you install it somewhere the compiler and linker don't search by default, export `WOLFTPM_ROOT`
+so CMake picks up its headers and `libwolftpm`:
+
+```shell
+export WOLFTPM_ROOT=/opt/wolftpm
+```
+
+`TPM2_TCTI_TYPE` only applies to `TPM_STACK=tpm2`. wolfTPM uses the transport it was
+built with: with `--enable-devtpm` it opens `/dev/tpmrm0` (the kernel resource manager),
+falling back to `/dev/tpm0`. Because `/dev/tpm0` is held exclusively by `tpm2-abrmd` when
+that service is running, make sure `/dev/tpmrm0` is available and that your user can open
+it (typically membership of the `tss` group).
+
+> ***NOTE***: TPM provisioning is not yet backend-independent. `TPM_STACK=wolftpm` covers
+> the *runtime* attestation path (signing during TO1/TO2) only. The one-time provisioning
+> step, [`utils/tpm_make_ready_ecdsa.sh`](../utils/tpm_make_ready_ecdsa.sh), still generates
+> the device CSR with `openssl req -provider tpm2`, so it requires OpenSSL 3 and the
+> `tpm2-openssl` provider (plus the `tpm2-tools` package) even when the SDK itself is built
+> with `TLS=wolfssl` and `TPM_STACK=wolftpm`. Do not pass `-s` to
+> [`utils/install_tpm_libs.sh`](../utils/install_tpm_libs.sh) if you intend to provision on
+> the same machine. A wolfCrypt-native replacement is tracked as a TODO in that script.
+
+## 9. CSE* Support
+
+Build with `-DDA=cse_ecdsa384`, same as the [CSE reference implementation](cse.md). The CSE
+implementation (`crypto/common/cse_crypto_support.c`, reused as-is) talks to Intel's Converged
+Security Engine over HECI and has no crypto-backend dependency. It still needs the Intel
+platform hardware/firmware with the FDO CSE client enabled, see [docs/cse.md](cse.md) for
+prerequisites, which apply regardless of `TLS=` backend.
+
+## 10. Licensing
+
+The FDO Client SDK is Apache-2.0, but wolfSSL and wolfTPM are distributed under **GPLv3**
+(with a commercial license available from wolfSSL Inc.), unlike the Apache-2.0 OpenSSL 3
+used by the default backend. Building with `TLS=wolfssl` — or with `TPM_STACK=wolftpm`
+under any TLS backend — links GPLv3 code into `linux-client`, which carries obligations for
+anything you redistribute. See the [NOTICE](../NOTICE) file for the full terms, including
+the GPLv2 relicensing exception and commercial licensing contact.
